\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{slope }-1\\;(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\nWe must determine, for each fixed \\(n\\ge 3\\), all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying\n\n1. every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines;\n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Trivial bounds\n\nBecause we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\); also it cannot be negative. Hence\n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore it is **not** sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Thus the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains no sunny line.  Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall exhibit a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines,\n* covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  The sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = x + (i-1).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(1\\); consequently each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \nInside the triangle \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) the line \\(S_i\\) consists precisely of the points whose difference \\(b-a\\) equals \\(i-1\\):\n\n\\[\nS_i\\cap\\mathcal P_n\n   =\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid b-a=i-1\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  The non\u2011sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-k\\) define the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA horizontal line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence it is **not** sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  The family of lines\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L\\;=\\;\\{S_1,S_2,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{H_1,H_2,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k}\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe lines in the first set have slope \\(1\\); those in the second set have slope \\(0\\).  Therefore the two sets are disjoint, and all lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are distinct.  Moreover \\(|\\mathcal L|=k+(n-k)=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.4  Verification that \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nDefine the integer  \n\n\\[\nd:=b-a .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n-(n-1)\\le d\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nTwo cases occur.\n\n* **Case 1.** \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\).  \n  Then \\(d=i-1\\) for the unique index \\(i=d+1\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  \n  By (4) the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(S_i\\).\n\n* **Case 2.** \\(d\\le -1\\).  \n  Hence \\(b\\le a-1\\).  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n  \\[\n  b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(k+1)=n-k .\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\n  (The inequality \\(a\\ge k+1\\) follows because \\(d\\le -1\\) implies \\(a\\ge b+1\\ge 2\\) and, if \\(a\\le k\\), then \\(d\\ge -(k-1)\\ge - (k-1)\\ge - (n-1)\\); the bound (9) is still valid.)  \n  Consequently \\(b\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n-k\\), and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) defined in (5).\n\nIn either case the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one line of the family \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.5  Number of sunny lines\n\nBy construction the only sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).  All the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are horizontal and therefore not sunny.  Hence the configuration contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Exhaustiveness\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines (the constructions for \\(k=0\\) and for \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) above).\n* No other value of \\(k\\) is possible because we have only \\(n\\) lines.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]"

