            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary condition\n\nBecause we must use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Extremal constructions\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,n)\n  \\]\n\n  are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and for any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) lines  \n\n  \\[\n  S_i:\\;y = x + (i-1)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n  \\]\n\n  all have slope \\(1\\) (therefore sunny) and are distinct because their intercepts differ.  \n  For \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) put \\(i=b-a+1\\); the inequalities \\(1\\le a,b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) give \\(1\\le i\\le n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Reduction Lemma\n\nLet \\(m\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le m\\le n\\).  \nConsider the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,m) .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with abscissa \\(a\\le m\\) lies on one of the lines \\(V_j\\).  \nThe points that are not yet covered satisfy \\(a\\ge m+1\\).  \nFor such a point we have\n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(m+1)=n-m .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the set of uncovered points is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge m+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n =\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge 1,\\;a'+b'\\le (n-m)+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere we have written \\(a'=a-m\\) and \\(b'=b\\).  \nThus, after the translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-m,b)\\), the uncovered set is \\(\\mathcal P_{\\,n-m}\\).\n\nThe same statement holds if we replace the vertical lines (2) by the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Covering \\(\\mathcal P_t\\) with exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(t\\ge 1\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,t\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*The slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.*  \n*The slopes are pairwise different, so the lines are distinct.*\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 4.1**  \n\\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{i=1}^{t} L_i\\) contains every point of \\(\\mathcal P_t\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_t\\). Set  \n\n\\[\ni:=a+b-1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le t+1\\), we have \\(1\\le i\\le t\\).  \nMultiplying the equation of \\(L_i\\) by \\(i+1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(i+1)b = i a + 1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(i=a+b-1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(i+1)b = (a+b-1+1)b = (a+b)b = (a+b-1)a + a + b = i a + 1 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly (5). Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\) and lies on that line. ∎\n\nThus the \\(t\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{t}\\) cover the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_t\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(m=n-k\\).\n\n*Step 1 – non‑sunny lines.*  \nTake the \\(m\\) vertical lines \\(V_j\\) defined in (2).  \nThese are non‑sunny and together they cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose abscissa does not exceed \\(m\\).\n\n*Step 2 – reduction.*  \nBy Lemma 3 the points that are still uncovered form, after the translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-m,b)\\), the set \\(\\mathcal P_k\\).\n\n*Step 3 – sunny lines.*  \nCover this translated copy of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) with the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) given by (3).  \nUndoing the translation, each line \\(L_i\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\tilde L_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,(x-m) + \\frac{1}{i+1},\n\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k,\n\\]\n\nwhich is still sunny (the slope is unchanged) and distinct from the vertical lines \\(V_j\\).\n\n*Step 4 – verification.*  \nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) either has abscissa \\(\\le m\\) and is on some \\(V_j\\), or else, after subtracting \\(m\\) from its abscissa, belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) and therefore lies on one of the lines \\(\\tilde L_i\\) by Lemma 4.1.  \nConsequently the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered.\n\nThe total number of lines is \\(m+k=n\\); exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\tilde L_i\\) are sunny, while the \\(m\\) vertical lines are not. Hence we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Completion of the proof\n\nWe have exhibited explicit constructions for the two extremal values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\), and, using the reduction lemma together with Lemma 4.1, we have built a configuration for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
