eger \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a required\n   configuration exists. No other values of \\(k\\) are possible because the number of sunny lines\n   cannot exceed the total number of lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\qquad (n\\ge 3).\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\nThus a sunny line has a slope belonging to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_3\\) consists of the three points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n\\]\n\nThe following families of three distinct lines cover these points and contain the indicated\nnumber \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) (all non‑sunny).\n\n* \\(k=1\\): the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2\\) together with the sunny line\n  \\(y=x-1\\) (slope \\(1\\)), which passes through \\((2,1)\\).\n\n* \\(k=2\\): the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny) and the two sunny lines\n  \\(y=x-1\\) (through \\((2,1)\\)) and \\(y=2x-1\\) (through \\((1,1)\\)).\n\n* \\(k=3\\): three distinct sunny lines, e.g.  \n  \\(y=x-1\\) (through \\((2,1)\\)), \\(y=2x-1\\) (through \\((1,1)\\)),\n  and \\(y=3x-2\\) (through \\((1,2)\\)).\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive hypothesis\n\nAssume that for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following holds:\n\n> (**H\\(_n\\)**)  \n> For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family\n> \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> (i) every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of the family, and  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Inductive step\n\nWe prove (**H\\(_{n+1}\\)**).  \nLet \\(k'\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k'\\le n+1\\).  \nWe construct a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k'}\\) of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines satisfying the\nrequired properties.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  The case \\(k'\\le n\\)\n\nTake the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k'}\\) supplied by the hypothesis (**H\\(_n\\)**).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) are already covered.  \nThe set \\(T_{n+1}\\) differs from \\(T_n\\) only by the single new point  \n\n\\[\nP_{new}=(n+1,1),\n\\qquad\\text{since }(a,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\setminus T_n\\Longleftrightarrow a=n+1,\\;b=1 .\n\\]\n\nAdd to \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k'}\\) the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{new}: x=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_{new}\\) is **non‑sunny** (vertical), it is distinct from all lines of\n\\(\\mathcal L_{n,k'}\\) (none of those lines has equation \\(x=n+1\\)), and it passes through\n\\(P_{new}\\).  \nConsequently the enlarged family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k'}:=\\mathcal L_{n,k'}\\cup\\{\\ell_{new}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\), and still has exactly\n\\(k'\\) sunny lines (the added line is non‑sunny).  \n\nThus a configuration exists for every \\(k'\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  The case \\(k'=n+1\\)\n\nHere we need **all** lines to be sunny.  \nStart from the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) given by (**H\\(_n\\)**); it consists of \\(n\\) distinct\nsunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\nAgain the only uncovered point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) is \\(P_{new}=(n+1,1)\\).\nChoose a real number \\(m\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nm\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\cup\\{\\text{slope of any line of }\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set on the right is finite, hence such an \\(m\\) exists.  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{new}:\\; y-1=m\\,(x-(n+1)).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_{new}\\) is \\(m\\); therefore \\(\\ell_{new}\\) is sunny and, by the choice of\n\\(m\\), it is distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\).  \nMoreover \\(\\ell_{new}\\) passes through \\(P_{new}\\).\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,n+1}:=\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\cup\\{\\ell_{new}\\}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\nThus a configuration exists for \\(k'=n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the induction\n\nThe two sub‑cases together show that, assuming (**H\\(_n\\)**), we can construct a family\n\\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k'}\\) for **every** integer \\(k'\\) with \\(0\\le k'\\le n+1\\).  \nSince (**H\\(_3\\)**) holds (Section 1), the induction yields (**H\\(_n\\)**) for all\n\\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nBy definition the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines.\nHence for a given \\(n\\) we must have \\(k\\le n\\).  \nThe constructions above show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are precisely the\nnon‑negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\).**"
