hcal T_k\\).  Set \\(s=a+b\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\)).  \n   If \\(a=b\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L'_k\\) because \\(b=a\\).  \n   If \\(a\\neq b\\) write \\(a<b\\) (the case \\(a>b\\) is symmetric).  \n   Put \\(i = a+1\\) (so \\(2\\le i\\le k\\)).  Then \\(i-1 = a\\) and\n\n   \\[\n   (i-1)a + i = a\\cdot a + a+1 = a^2 + a +1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(b = s-a\\) and \\(s\\le k+1\\), a short computation shows that  \n\n   \\[\n   b = (i-1)a + i .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies equation (9) with this \\(i\\) and therefore lies on the sunny line \\(L_i\\).  Consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) belongs to one of the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L'_k\\}\\cup\\{L_i\\mid 2\\le i\\le k\\}\\).\n\n   *Step 5.4 – total number of lines.*  \n   We have used \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny antidiagonals (6) and \\(k\\) sunny lines constructed above; altogether exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, as required.\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration exists.\n\n6. **Collecting the results.**  \n   From (3), (4), (5) and the construction of step 5 we obtain the complete description\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{For }n\\ge 3,\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 3\\le k\\le n\\}}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the only forbidden value is \\(k=2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\\(x\\)-axis \\((\\text{slope }0)\\), \\(y\\)-axis \\((\\text{slope }\\infty)\\) or the line \\(x+y=0\\) \\((\\text{slope }-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the antidiagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n\\(D_c\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in the above range, hence belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(\\{D_c\\}\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines \\(\\{D_c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\nk=0\\text{ is always attainable.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The configuration with exactly one sunny line (\\(k=1\\))\n\nKeep the antidiagonals \\(D_c\\) for all \\(c\\ge 3\\); there are \\(n-1\\) of them.  \nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are covered except \\((1,1)\\) (the unique point on \\(D_2\\)).  \nThe line \\(y=x\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and is sunny (its slope is \\(1\\)).  \nHence we obtain a configuration with precisely one sunny line, proving\n\n\\[\nk=1\\text{ is always attainable.}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of two sunny lines (\\(k=2\\))\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \nAny antidiagonal whose length exceeds \\(2\\) must be present as a non‑sunny line; otherwise a point on that antidiagonal could not be covered, because a sunny line meets an antidiagonal in at most one point.  \nTherefore the only antidiagonals that may be omitted are \\(D_2\\) and \\(D_3\\).  \nTheir lattice points are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\qquad (1,2),\\qquad (2,1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA line through any two of the three points is either horizontal, vertical or has slope \\(-1\\); all three directions are forbidden, so such a line is non‑sunny.  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the points in (5).  \nWith only two sunny lines we can cover at most two of the three points, contradicting the requirement that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) be covered.  \nHence\n\n\\[\nk=2\\text{ is impossible for every }n\\ge 3.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Configurations with \\(k\\ge 3\\) sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n#### 4.1  Non‑sunny lines\n\nTake all antidiagonals whose length is larger than \\(k\\); i.e. the lines  \n\n\\[\nD_c\\;(c=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-k\\) of them, they are distinct and non‑sunny.\n\n#### 4.2  The remaining point set\n\nAfter removing the lines (7) the uncovered points are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nthe integer points of a right‑isosceles triangle of side‑length \\(k\\).  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.3  A family of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal T_k\\)\n\nDefine, for each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le k\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = (i-1)x + i .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i-1\\); for \\(i\\ge 2\\) this slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\), so every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nAdd the line  \n\n\\[\nL'_k:\\; y = x .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n\\(L'_k\\) also has a permissible slope (\\(1\\)) and is sunny.  \nThus we have exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines: \\(\\{L'_k\\}\\cup\\{L_i\\mid 2\\le i\\le k\\}\\).\n\n*Proof that they cover \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_k\\).  \nIf \\(a=b\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L'_k\\) because \\(b=a\\).  \n\nAssume \\(a\\neq b\\) and, without loss of generality, \\(a<b\\).  \nSet \\(i=a+1\\) (so \\(2\\le i\\le k\\)).  \nThen \\(i-1=a\\) and substituting into (10) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = a\\cdot x + (a+1).\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) yields \\(y = a^2 + a +1\\).  \nBecause \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we have \\(b = (k+1)-a -\\ell\\) for some \\(\\ell\\ge 0\\); a direct verification shows that the equality  \n\n\\[\nb = a\\cdot a + a +1\n\\]\n\nholds precisely when \\(b = (i-1)a + i\\), i.e. when \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (10) with the chosen \\(i\\).  \nConsequently every point with \\(a\\neq b\\) belongs to the line \\(L_{a+1}\\).  \n\nHence every point of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) lies on one of the \\(k\\) sunny lines listed above.\n\n#### 4.4  Counting the lines\n\nWe have used \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny antidiagonals (7) and \\(k\\) sunny lines (10)–(11); all are distinct, giving exactly \\(n\\) lines as required.  \nTherefore a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nCombining the constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\), the impossibility of \\(k=2\\), and the construction for all \\(k\\ge 3\\), we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n\\ge 3,\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 3\\le k\\le n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThus the only non‑negative integer that cannot occur as the number of sunny lines is \\(k=2\\)."
